  "body": "This repository continues the history of `terraform-google-spine-liquor` under the product's current name. The module publishes to the Terraform Registry as `SpineEventEngine/delivery/google`, which is the path the `delivery` repository's README already links to.\n\n## Renames\n\n- The VPC is `delivery`; the VM and the Docker container are `delivery-server`; the instance template prefix is `delivery-<region>`.\n- The `liquor_network` module label is `delivery_network`. The `liquor` output, which holds the VM name, is `server`, so a consumer reads it as `module.delivery.server`.\n- Descriptions, comments, and the README no longer mention Liquor, apart from the note on migrating from the `spine-liquor` module.\n\n## Artifact Registry\n\n- The README example deploys `europe-docker.pkg.dev/spine-event-engine/containers/delivery-server`, the public image the `delivery` repository publishes. The startup script configures the Docker credential helper for that registry host instead of the retired `gcr.io`.\n- The README describes the single server image. The notes contrasting the \"simple-server\" and \"server\" containers and their version gates are gone. The default admin port is documented as `8080`, which is what the module opens when no port is given.\n\n## Firewall default\n\n- The Admin server port is opened only when `admin.enabled` is true, on the configured port or 8080 when none is given. Previously TCP 8080 was opened by default even with the Admin server disabled, which is the default. Raised in review.\n- `moved` blocks map the renamed VM and network for deployments migrating from the `spine-liquor` module, so Terraform plans an ordered replacement rather than an unrelated creation. Raised in review.\n- The startup script configures the Docker credential helper for whichever Google registry hosts the image, and for none otherwise. Raised in review.\n\n## Dependency upgrade\n\n| Dependency | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Terraform | `>= 1.0.0` | `>= 1.3.0` |\n| `google` provider | `>= 3.86.0, < 4.0.0` (locked 3.89.0) | `>= 6.28.0, < 8.0.0` (locked 7.46.1) |\n| `terraform-google-modules/vm` instance template | `~> 7.1` (7.9.0) | `~> 15.2` (15.2.1) |\n| `terraform-google-modules/network` and its firewall rules | `~> 3.4` (3.5.0) | `~> 18.2` (18.2.0) |\n\n- The Terraform floor was never accurate: the `admin` input uses `optional()` attributes, which need 1.3.\n- Provider 8.x exists, but both upstream modules cap at `< 8`.\n- The firewall rules move from the deprecated `rules` input to `ingress_rules`. Their names derive from `var.vpc_name` instead of the network module's output, because version 18 keeps that output unknown until apply and the names key the `for_each` of the firewall resources.\n- The lock files inside the child modules are removed; Terraform reads only the root one, and they still pinned 3.89.0.\n- `terraform fmt` is applied to the pre-existing files, and the README documents the requirements.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `terraform validate` and `terraform fmt -check -recursive` pass.\n- `terraform plan` against `spine-event-engine` with placeholder inputs: 7 to add with the Admin server disabled, 8 with it enabled (the custom rule opens 8080, or the configured port), 0 to change, 0 to destroy, no warnings. The template shows the intended machine type, disk, image family, and Shielded VM settings; the rendered startup script pulls the Artifact Registry image and names the container `delivery-server`.\n- Not exercised: `terraform apply`. The firewall module's internal resource addresses changed between its versions 3 and 18, so an existing Liquor deployment moved onto this module would see its rules recreated; fresh Delivery deployments are unaffected.\n\n## For reviewers\n\n- The README example pins module version `0.11.0` and image tag `v0.19.0`. Neither exists yet: the module version is whatever tag follows this merge, and the image tag appears once SpineEventEngine/delivery#67 is merged and published.\n- The upstream module versions keep the repository's floating `~>` style. Exact pins would make every `init` reproducible at the cost of manual bumps.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
